// embers — the first kernel: a rill mounted on a spray.
//
// A fountain of rows thrown along the spray's aim, arcing under gravity,
// retired when they run out of life. That is the whole file, and it is
// deliberately the whole file: this is the smallest thing that is a kernel.
//
// ⚠ There is no LOOK here — no colour, no size, no fade. What makes these
// read as embers is the spray's appearance, declared where the spray is,
// not here. Read as a behaviour, this is equally rain or confetti.
//
// Every line is an independent flow over the row. `spawn` launches a newborn
// along the spray's aim at the spray's speed; `gravity` bends every row's
// velocity by a broadcast knob, so a rill writing that path retunes every row
// next tick; `perish` retires a row when its age reaches its life.
// Integration is not a word: a velocity moves its position.
//
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`; everything else needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero. So:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/embers.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.gravity =<cells/s²>
//
// Units: `row.size` is a RADIUS in cells, `gravity` is cells/s² on y and
// NEGATIVE is down (a positive pull lifts).
//
// `life`, `rate`, `speed` and `spread` are the SPRAY's, not this file's —
// every "make them last longer / denser / faster / wider" note lands there.
spawn
gravity plane.drift.@self.k.gravity
perish
